# macOS / editor cruft
.DS_Store
*.textClipping

# Third-party course materials. The textbook chapter is a copyrighted work
# published by its publisher, not ours to redistribute — keep it locally, out
# of version control, even in a private repository.
Ch 1..pdf
Ch. 2.docx
Ch*.pdf
Ch*.docx
*.epub

# Institutional and assessment documents. These carry the school name, the
# course code, and the wording of a graded case. The module itself is written
# to contain none of that, and committing the source documents would put it
# all back. Keep them beside the repo, not inside it.
*Signature Assignment*.docx
Course Objectives*.docx
*Management-Information-Systems.pdf
Syllabus*.pdf

# The supplied course materials, kept beside the working copy rather than in it. Everything in here is either
# the publisher's chapter text or institutional paperwork: the textbook chapters, the eleven course objectives,
# and the signature assignment whose case name is the reserved term src/check.mjs --release refuses to ship.
# Ignoring the directory rather than each file means a new document dropped in here cannot be swept in by a
# `git add -A` before anyone notices it.
book/

# Terms reserved to a live assessment, checked by src/check.mjs but never
# committed — naming them here would be the same leak by another route.
src/forbidden.local.txt

# Instructor-only grading guidance for the practice case study. The student-facing page sits right
# beside this file, so keep the model-answer notes out of git rather than trust a manual review of
# every future `git add` to catch it. Commit only when explicitly released.
practice-case-study/instructor-notes*.md

# The live midterm and its answer key. This repository is public and the exam is closed book and
# proctored, so the paper itself stays out as well as the key — practice-midterm.html is the
# student-facing rehearsal, and practice-midterm.test.mjs is written to skip the comparison when
# the exam is absent, which is the normal state here. The page carries no answers either way.
midterm.html
midterm.test.mjs
midterm-instructor-key*.md
midterm-answer-key*.md
practice-midterm-instructor-key*.md
practice-midterm-answer-key*.md

# The Week 1 instructor source: the answer key, the Blackboard question text, the SQL solutions, and
# the expected/ fixtures that drive the browser suite. build.mjs reads this directory to assemble the
# instructor archive; nothing here may ever be committed. It was restored from that archive on
# 2026-09-15 after the archive itself was purged from history, and it showed up as plain untracked
# files — one `git add -A` away from republishing everything the purge removed.
hw/instructor/
hw/**/ANSWER-KEY.md
hw/**/BLACKBOARD-TEXT.md
hw/**/solutions-ch*.sql

# Instructor archives. This one was tracked from 7e1f3e0 until 2026-09-15 carrying ANSWER-KEY.md,
# the Blackboard text and three .sql solution files. Every other leak check in this repository works
# on file names, and a .zip is one opaque blob to all of them — so the rule is by name here, and
# src/check-archives.mjs looks inside whatever is still tracked.
hw/Week1InstructorMaterials.zip
*InstructorMaterials*.zip
*instructor*.zip

# The homework answer keys, for the same reason: each page is committed and its key sits in the
# same directory. The pages themselves carry no answers at all, only a weights comment, because a
# student can read the source of anything handed to them.
homework/homework-06-instructor-key*.md
homework/homework-07-instructor-key*.md
homework/*-answer-key*.md

# The Blackboard import file is the whole exam with its answers marked, in plain
# text. It and everything built beside it stay out of a public repository.
blackboard/

# Build scratch
/tmp/
*.tmp.html

# The Chapter 8 game's solutions. It carries every right answer, every designed wrong answer, the
# partial-credit ladders and the reference arithmetic, so on a public repository it is an answer key
# like any other. The game itself teaches without it — every option explains itself the moment it is
# answered — so this is for whoever is running the session, not for the people playing it. Commit
# only if that judgement is ever deliberately reversed.
games/rocks-in-the-river-solutions.html
